WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.431
We have seen ipconfig used in some of the exam topics covered in this course. Let's take a moment to focus solely on ipconfig as a command-line tool.

00:09.431 --> 00:24.103
Simply put, ipconfig gives us IP address information. If I run ipconfig by itself, we see IP address information for each network adapter on this device.

00:24.103 --> 00:41.028
If we run ipconfig /all, we get further information, such as the DHCP server and DNS server or servers for each network adapter.

00:41.028 --> 00:47.943
Two more ipconfig commands we can run are ipconfig /release and ipconfig /renew.

00:47.943 --> 00:56.792
I don't want to run ipconfig /release right now because doing so will release these IP addresses and I will lose my network connectivity.

00:56.792 --> 01:12.759
ipconfig /renew is used when you need to try to obtain an IP address. I will run this command, though: ipconfig /displaydns. I'll press Enter.

01:12.759 --> 01:20.959
This displays the DNS server addresses for all of the sites I have recently visited.

01:20.959 --> 01:41.720
And, if for whatever reason you can't get to websites by name and your device has recently changed networks, you can run ipconfig /flushdns to empty the DNS cache.

